fact WorkItem(id: "WI-995", description: some(value: "An import is scoped to the ADDRESS, not to the file it is written in — so one file changes how names resolve in another.\n\nTHE RULE (user decision, 2026-08-04): an import resolves ONLY in the file where it\nis listed, and it is SPENT BEFORE any merging. An import maps a local name to a\nSymbol; once names are Symbols it has no further job.\n\nTHE DIVERGENCE. `process_imports` writes into the table keyed by SCOPE, and a\nscope is the address: `namespace X` and `sort X` both alloc `Fn{X,[],[]}`, which\nhash-conses to one TermId, so every text writing into one address shares one\nimport table. Fixtures A-D (see history) measured drive() = 1/2/1/2; C names the\nreach (the address, not the text), D places the ticket (plain namespace-level\nimport scoping, no sort at the address, so NOT 059's secondary entries).\n\nMEASURED, 2026-08-11 — the cost the ticket demanded before any behaviour change.\n\nINSTRUMENT (landed, default-off). `SymbolTable` now records WHO WROTE each import:\n`ImportOrigin::{Builtin, File(idx), Declaration}` on both the alias table\n(`import_origin`) and the import-contributed parent links (`import_parent_origin`).\n`resolve_in_scope` answers twice while auditing — as today, and under\n`ImportVisibility::OwnFileOnly` — and records the disagreements. A counterfactual,\nnot a proxy: counting import statements or shared addresses measures exposure, not\nwhether any name actually reaches across.\n`ANTHILL_FILE_LOCAL_IMPORTS=1` ENFORCES the rule instead of auditing it.\n\nRESULT 1 — THE .anthill CORPUS COSTS ZERO. Six groups (stdlib alone; +rust host\nbindings; +github-todo; +anthill-todo; +webots-modelling; +anthill-testcases),\n69-94 files, 10.9k-24.1k resolutions each: 0 cross-file dependencies in every one.\nWHY: 238 of 250 imports are SELECTIVE (which add no parent link), 12 plain, 0\nwildcard, and 0 top-level imports — so nothing lands in the shared `_global` table.\nThe one shared-address duplicate is instructive: stdlib/anthill/geometry.anthill\nand rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/geometry.anthill BOTH write\n`import anthill.prelude.{Float}` into `namespace anthill.geometry` — duplicated\nbecause each file imports what it uses, which is the discipline the rule mandates.\n\nRESULT 2 — THE SUITE COSTS 43 TESTS (4440 passed / 43 failed, vs 4483 / 0 at\nbaseline; 29 binaries both runs, so neither masks a compile error). The corpus zero\nis honest but does not reach what the fixtures reach. TWO causes, only one a cost:\n\n  (a) IMPORTS STOP RE-EXPORTING — 12 tests (wi844_sorted_set_driver_test).\n      stdlib/anthill/prelude/int64.anthill:7 writes\n      `import anthill.prelude.Numeric.{add, sub}` INSIDE the Int64 scope, and the\n      fixture writes `import anthill.prelude.Int64.{sub}` — importing a name that\n      is in Int64 only because Int64's own file imported it there. Under the rule:\n      `unresolved import 'anthill.prelude.Int64.sub'`. This is the ticket's own\n      anticipated case, it is CORRECT (imports are not re-exports, as in most\n      languages), and the repair is per-site: `Int64.{sub}` -> `Numeric.{sub}`.\n\n  (b) THE QUERY PATH HAS NO FILE TO BE LOCAL TO — 31 tests (wi853, wi907, wi908,\n      wi914, wi916, wi917, wi919). NOT a migration cost — a design gap that must\n      close before the rule can land. `-i <ns>` synthesizes its OWN single-file\n      source containing `import <ns>`, then the query resolves at `_global` where\n      no file is asking, so a strict rule blinds every query to its own `-i` flags:\n      \"'mk' in query pattern does not resolve to a known functor\".\n\nTWO PREMISES OF THIS TICKET WERE WRONG, both found by reading the code:\n  - AN IMPORT WRITES TWO THINGS. Beyond `Scope.imports` (one reader), `Plain` and\n    `Wildcard` imports call `add_parent`, whose list is SHARED with `requires` and\n    enclosing links. Suppressing an edge on a foreign import's say-so would also\n    suppress it where a `requires` independently justifies it — hence\n    `ImportOrigin::Declaration`, recorded by every non-import `add_parent`.\n  - \"SPENT BEFORE MERGING\" IS NOT TRUE TODAY. `Scope.imports` survives the load:\n    the typer (typing.rs) and `anthill query` both resolve through it. Deleting the\n    table after load is NOT a valid implementation of the rule.\n\nWHERE THE FILE IDENTITY COMES FROM — settled, and it needs no new carrier.\n`SourceSpan { source: SourceId, span }` already IS \"a span with file identity\", and\n`NodeOccurrence.span` is a `SourceSpan`, so every occurrence already knows its file;\n`Loader.source_id` is fixed for the whole file. File must NOT go on `Term`/`TermId`\n(it would break hash-consing — two identical terms in two files would stop sharing\na TermId) nor on `TermView` (a file is not a carrier property). It is a property of\na SITE. The gap is only that the SYMBOL TABLE recorded no writer; that is now closed.\n\nDELIVERED 2026-08-11 (backward compatibility explicitly NOT a constraint — user).\nThe rule is the DEFAULT; `ANTHILL_FILE_LOCAL_IMPORTS` existed only to measure and is\ndeleted. Full suite 4483 passed / 0 failed, 29 binaries, 0 compile errors.\n\nWHAT THE FILE IDENTITY IS. `SourceId` — the identity `SourceSpan` already carries, so\nan occurrence names its own file with no lookup table. Source registration is HOISTED\nahead of `scan_definitions` (`register_sources`), so the scan, the declaration pass and\nthe load pass share one id per file. A load-slice INDEX was tried first and is wrong:\n`load_incremental`'s phase 2 and the CLI's query scan each index from 0 again, so a\nslice position silently aliases one file onto another.\n\nTHE ASKING FILE. `SymbolTable::set_asking_file` is ambient during the load, which is\nhonest there — a `Loader` IS a file (`Loader.source_id` is fixed for the whole file).\nThe TYPER needed nothing, on evidence rather than assumption: its one `resolve_in_scope`\nis gated on `is_type_param`, always a scope local, and the enforced run produced zero\ntyper-side failures with the asking file `None` throughout typing.\n\n`ImportOrigin::Invocation` — the channel for a FILE-LESS asker. `anthill query -i <ns>`\nparses its flag from a synthetic one-line source; attributing that to a \"file\" made the\nflag local to it and so invisible to the very query it was passed to serve. A flag\nbelongs to the RUN, like the prelude's own aliases. `common::supply_invocation_imports`\nis the in-process spelling, used by the fixtures below.\n\nTHE 26 SITES REPAIRED, and none needed a mechanism redesign:\n  - wi844 (12): ONE line — `Int64.{sub}` -> `Numeric.{sub}`. Passes under BOTH the old\n    and new rule, so it is a strict improvement, not a compatibility shim.\n  - wi907 (3), wi917 (3), wi919 (1), wi908 (2), wi916 (4): imports moved to the\n    invocation channel. These fixtures' own comments said they were modelling `-i` all\n    along (\"`-i <ns>.*` twice is the CLI spelling\"). A MOUNT never needed an import to\n    supply a namespace — it takes a fully qualified name and always did (verified) —\n    so the earlier claim that persistence needed a new import channel was WRONG.\n  - wi853 (1): INVERTED, and its module header rewritten rather than deleted. It taught\n    top-level import sharing as a deliberate difference from \"the file-local import of\n    most languages\", reasoning the language \"has no per-file scope to attach a narrower\n    one to\". This ticket supplies that scope. An assertion was ADDED that a top-level\n    DEFINITION still crosses files, so the refusal is pinned as being about imports and\n    not about `_global` going private.\n\nWI-916'S HAZARD IS NARROWER NOW, stated at its site. It built its contest by loading a\nsecond FILE whose top-level import contested the mount name for everyone; that route is\nclosed by construction. The contest now comes through a second `-i` after the mount — a\ngenuine sequence — so the defect stays live and the Symbol-keying fix still stops it.\n\nSPEC UPDATED (`docs/kernel-language.md`): the \"Where an import may be written\" passage\n(which stated the OPPOSITE rule), the `resolve_in_scope` step 2, the import-forms list\n(both things an import writes), and the implicit-namespace merging bullet.\n\nTHE AUDIT SURVIVES THE FLIP, inverted: it now resolves the PRE-RULE way and reports\nwhere that disagrees, so it keeps measuring instead of trivially agreeing with itself.\n\nREVIEW ROUND (/code-review high) FOUND A REAL HOLE IN THE FIX, worth recording because\nthe delivered control could not catch it.\n\n  THE RULE WAS KEYED ON THE NAME, NOT THE SYMBOL. `Scope.imports` is a `HashMap`\n  (last write wins). Step 1b asked only \"did SOME visible origin write an entry under\n  this name\" and then returned the MAP's winner — so with file A writing\n  `import a.{X}` and file B `import b.{X}` at one address, A read B's `X`, decided by\n  load order. That is the very non-locality this ticket removes, surviving inside its\n  own implementation. `visible_import` now returns the visible WRITE's own symbol out\n  of `import_origin` (last visible write wins, preserving in-file last-write-wins).\n\n  WHY FIXTURE D MISSED IT, and the lesson: D's foreign import is the ONLY write of\n  that name, so name-keying and symbol-keying AGREE there. A one-writer fixture cannot\n  distinguish \"which name\" from \"whose name\". `wi995_two_files_importing_one_name_each_\n  read_their_own` drives the two-writer case (distinct field labels, so a mix-up is a\n  closed-label load error; both load orders) and was VERIFIED to fail with the fix\n  backed out — while fixture D passed throughout that back-out.\n\n  `contested_import_entries` had the same defect from the other side: it filtered on\n  ORIGIN difference, so it flagged two files importing the SAME target (harmless) and\n  missed one file importing two DIFFERENT targets under one name. Now filters on symbol.\n\nFIVE MORE, all fixed: the public `scan_definitions` cleared the asking file, so the\nshipped scan-then-RESOLVE path (`anthill query`) made the scanned source's own imports\ninvisible to the resolution that followed — it now leaves it set; the instrument's own\n`alias_entries` guard counted `Builtin` prelude aliases and so would have passed on a\nrun that recorded no file-written import at all; a helper was inserted between\n`mount_extent`'s doc/attribute and `mount_extent`; a dropped negation inverted a spec\nsentence; and the per-edge origin probe is now gated on a per-scope flag, so the scopes\nwith no file-scoped import parent (all but ~10 in the stdlib) answer with a field read.\n\nNOT TAKEN: moving the origin onto `ScopeInclusion` to drop the side table. Its derived\n`PartialEq` is `add_parent`'s dedup key and WI-994 established that a scope's parents\nare a SET; an origin field would split links differing only by writer and silently undo\nthat. The per-scope flag buys the same lookup cost without touching the key.\n\nFINAL: 4484 passed / 0 failed, 29 binaries, 0 compile errors.\n\nNOT DONE: scaland has the same shape and should follow.\n\nACCEPTANCE MET: an import written in one file does not affect resolution in another,\ndriven by `wi995_control_a_foreign_files_import_is_detected` (fixture D refuses AND is\nreported; same-file keeps working; the wildcard path covered separately because an\nimport writes BOTH an alias and a parent link, suppressed by different predicates);\ncorpus cost measured and reported by `wi995_measure_import_file_locality_cost`;\ncargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-11T06:48:59Z"))
